{
 "war": "third-crusade",
 "start": "1189-05-11",
 "end": "1192-10-09",
 "note": "Crusader holdings on the coast and in Cyprus are drawn as occupied areas over the base map; a moving circle marks the ground held by a marching army, not a conquest. Areas and arrows are approximate reconstructions from campaign maps and the chronicles.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "barbarossa",
   "title": "Barbarossa's march",
   "start": "1189-05-11",
   "end": "1190-06-10",
   "bbox": [
    22.0,
    36.0,
    34.5,
    43.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Barbarossa's army marches from Regensburg to Philippopolis (May–August 1189)",
    "Barbarossa crosses to Asia and marches to Iconium (March–May 1190)",
    "The German army marches on to the Saleph river (June 1190)"
   ],
   "text": "Saladin's victory at Hattin in July 1187 and his capture of Jerusalem that October shocked Europe. The old emperor Frederick Barbarossa set out first, in May 1189, with a large German army; quarrelling with Byzantium, he occupied Philippopolis on 24 August and wintered at Adrianople. In 1190 he crossed into Asia, beat the Turks of Rum at Philomelion and stormed their capital Iconium on 18 May, but on 10 June he drowned in the Saleph river and most of his army went home."
  },
  {
   "id": "acre",
   "title": "The siege of Acre",
   "start": "1189-08-28",
   "end": "1191-07-12",
   "bbox": [
    34.85,
    32.55,
    35.65,
    33.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "King Guy marches from Tyre to Acre (August 1189)",
    "Richard sails from Cyprus to Acre (June 1191)"
   ],
   "text": "By 1189 Saladin held almost all of the Kingdom of Jerusalem; Tyre, defended by Conrad of Montferrat, was the main city left. In August 1189 King Guy marched south and laid siege to Acre, and Saladin's army in turn surrounded the besiegers. For two years both camps suffered battle, famine and disease while crusaders arrived by sea. Philip II of France came in April 1191 and Richard I of England in June, and Acre surrendered on 12 July 1191."
  },
  {
   "id": "cyprus",
   "title": "Richard takes Cyprus",
   "start": "1191-05-01",
   "end": "1191-06-05",
   "bbox": [
    32.1,
    34.4,
    34.8,
    35.8
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Richard's fleet lands at Limassol (May 1191)"
   ],
   "text": "On the way east, part of Richard's fleet was wrecked on Cyprus and its ruler, Isaac Komnenos, held the survivors. Richard landed and took Limassol in early May 1191 and married Berengaria of Navarre there on 12 May. Isaac's barons deserted him and he surrendered by the end of the month, and Richard sailed on to Acre on 5 June. Cyprus became a crusader base for centuries."
  },
  {
   "id": "arsuf",
   "title": "From Acre to Jaffa",
   "start": "1191-08-22",
   "end": "1191-09-30",
   "bbox": [
    34.4,
    31.85,
    35.45,
    33.1
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Richard marches down the coast from Acre to Jaffa (August–September 1191)",
    "Saladin's army shadows the crusaders inland (August–September 1191)"
   ],
   "text": "Philip went home after Acre fell, and Richard, now in command, executed about 2,700 Muslim prisoners when the ransom was delayed. On 22 August 1191 he marched south along the coast with the fleet beside him, while Saladin's army harried the column from inland. At Arsuf on 7 September Richard's knights charged and broke Saladin's army, and on 10 September the crusaders reached Jaffa, the port for Jerusalem."
  },
  {
   "id": "jerusalem",
   "title": "Within sight of Jerusalem",
   "start": "1191-10-01",
   "end": "1192-07-15",
   "bbox": [
    34.2,
    31.25,
    35.5,
    32.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "First advance on Jerusalem, to Beit Nuba (November 1191 – January 1192)",
    "Withdrawal to Ascalon (January 1192)",
    "Crusaders take Darum (May 1192)",
    "Second advance to Beit Nuba (June 1192)"
   ],
   "text": "In winter 1191 Richard advanced inland and reached Beit Nuba, about 20 km from Jerusalem, but rain, cold and the fear of being cut off from the coast made him turn back in January 1192. His men rebuilt the walls of Ascalon, which Saladin had pulled down, and on 22 May took Darum on the road to Egypt. In June a second advance came within sight of Jerusalem and turned back again."
  },
  {
   "id": "jaffa",
   "title": "Jaffa and the treaty",
   "start": "1192-07-20",
   "end": "1192-10-09",
   "bbox": [
    34.3,
    31.4,
    35.6,
    33.4
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Saladin attacks Jaffa (27 July 1192)",
    "Richard sails from Acre to save Jaffa (July–August 1192)"
   ],
   "text": "As Richard prepared to leave, Saladin attacked Jaffa on 27 July 1192 and broke into the town, but Richard arrived by sea, waded ashore and drove his troops out; on 5 August a small crusader force beat off Saladin's counter-attack. Both sides were exhausted, and the Treaty of Jaffa, agreed on 1 or 2 September, left the crusaders the coast from Tyre to Jaffa, kept Jerusalem in Muslim hands with access for Christian pilgrims, and had Ascalon's walls demolished. Richard sailed for home on 9 October."
  }
